/ khash/kcmpr on parser keeper nodes: AFL crash (fuzzer01 sig:11, x2),
/ 2026-07-18.  Two independent defects, both in src/k.core/k.c.
/
/ 1. NULL keeper slot.  p.c builds a 0xd0 fixed-dyad keeper as (seed;verb),
/    and the seed channel can be empty -- `b:(0[])0*` leaves 0xd0(NULL,0xc0'*').
/    khashcb strips the subtype and hands the payload back to khash as a plain
/    general list, where NULL reads as t0/no-subtype: the case-0 walk pushed it
/    and then deref'd it via n(x_) -> SIGSEGV (si_addr 0x0).  khash now folds a
/    NULL slot in as a constant and kcmpr orders it first, so equal values still
/    hash alike and collisions still resolve.
/    The b must be reached THROUGH a list -- a bare `?(b;1;b)` hashes the
/    keeper by a path that never walked the payload, so the nesting is load-
/    bearing, not incidental.
b:(0[])0*
?(,b;,b;,1)
/ same shape via a different primitive
c:(0[])0+
?(,c;,c;,1)
/ ordinary values still hash and compare unchanged (positive control)
?(,1;,1;,2;,1)
?(1 2;1 2;3 4)
=(,1;,2;,1)
(,1;,2)?/(,2;,1)
/ keeper values keep their identity under match/unique
b~b
b~c
#?(b;c;b)
/
/ 2. khash depth guard defeated.  khash released its depth counter BEFORE
/    calling khashcb (`--d; return khashcb(x)`), but khashcb re-enters khash
/    for the subtype payload -- so the mutual recursion ran at a constant d and
/    neither the maxr guard nor the FUZZING node budget (reset on d==1) could
/    ever fire.  A cyclic/deep subtyped value then blew the C stack at d~4078.
/    d is now held across the callback.  Deeply nested keeper lists must come
/    back with a value, not a crash:
d:,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,b
#?(d;d)
~[d;d]
/
/ 3. `.`` handed out the LIVE root (src/v.c).  ktree is the destination of
/    every global store, so a value referencing it becomes a cycle the moment
/    it is assigned -- the tree holds the name that holds the tree, and the
/    refcount GC can never reclaim it.  Explicit list construction copies a
/    shared dict, but adverb result collection SHARES, so the live root could
/    be captured by any of '  \  ''  '/  /'  \'  @'  (43 forms measured) and
/    stored.  Symptoms: !(*x) recursed to a stack error, and printing flooded
/    "kprint: type error" (kprint nulls the dict capacity slot across its
/    transpose, the walk re-enters dcp on that same dict, and dcp requires a
/    t1 capacity).  `.` now snapshots at the source, which also retired
/    scope_set_'s `v==ktree` gcopy -- that patch guarded the store side and
/    could not see a root nested inside a fresh container.
/    Two alternatives were measured and rejected: (a) giving ktree a refcount
/    so the generic "copy if shared" tests stop skipping it (ktree->r is 0, a C
/    global holds it with no reference) BREAKS t257 -- amend's CoW then sees the
/    tree as shared, copies it, and writes are lost; (b) adding the capture rule
/    to the adverb result paths fixed 41 of 43 forms but left the seeded-scan
/    ones, i.e. it needs every present and future adverb to remember.
/    The snapshot's .k lists the globals that existed when it was taken
/    (b, c, d above) and NOT x itself -- x's absence is the fix:
x:.'1#`
#x
!(*x)
!(*x)[`k]
/ same leak via a lambda result, and via the enlist route that always worked
y:{.`}'1#0
!(*y)
z:,.`
!(*z)
